title: The Death of Kornilov
subtitle: The President has died...
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1934 and month >= 7 and president = "Kornilov"
on-arrival: round = 0; kornilov_dead = 1; president = "Efremov (acting)"; president = "Zarudny (acting)" if judicial_reform >= 4; rsdrp_candidate = "Maisky"; rkp_candidate = "Kalinin"; rkp_candidate = "Milyutin" if rkp_party_leader = "Conciliators"; psr_candidate = "Chernov"; musavat_candidate = "Rasulzade"; nts_candidate = "Leuchtenberg"; nts_candidate = "Baidalakov" if leuchtenberg_deported; vs_candidate = "Romanov"; kadets_candidate = "Shingarev"; other_candidate = "Shingarev"; is_unity_candidate = 0; rsrpi_candidate = rkp_candidate;
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/kornilovdead.png
tags: event

= The Death of Kornilov

President Lavr Kornilov has passed away at age 64. An era of history is closing, and the future remains to be seen. He had reportedly contracted tubercolosis while visiting Ukraine, this does explain his unusual absence since he left office.

The nation engages in an outpouring of grief for its war hero and president, with even some sympathy among committed republicans and socialists. After all, Kornilov steered the republic through some of its most challenging times, both before and after the Civil War, without giving in to extremists on the far right. Perhaps he is one reason democracy remains alive.

New presidential elections are to be held as soon as possible. The Russian political landscape is wide open, with contenders from all over the political space. In the meantime, Supreme Court chief justice [? if judicial_reform < 4 : Ivan Efremov ?][? if judicial_reform >= 4 : Alexander Zarudny ?] serves as the acting president.

- @candidates: Who are the candidates so far?

@candidates
go-to: candidate_menu

[? if not leuchtenberg_deported : Of course, Sergey Leuchtenberg will be running for the Soldiarists, in his third run. We have kept him out of power so far, but nothing is certain. ?]
[? if leuchtenberg_deported : With Leuchtenberg deported, the Solidarists will be running Viktor Baidalakov as their candidate. ?]
This might be the Solidarist's last hurrah; the party's factionalism has been reaching new heights as of late, and it is in desperate shortage of funds.

The VS will nominate Michael Alexandrovich Romanov, the brother of Nicholas II who refused the throne. He will be endorsed by many of Kornilovs's Monarchist and Conservative supporters. They are working more closely with the Right-Liberals than before, and in the second round, the far-right parties are expected to consolidate into a single anti-socialist front.

[? if rkp_party_leader = "Conciliators" : Given the failure of Kalinin's previous runs for president, and his declining stature in the party, the RKP will be running Vladimir Milyutin, a controversial in the party sometimes called "Mr. Right". He is a Conciliator who leads a number of Communist front organizations. ?]
[? if rkp_party_leader != "Conciliators" : This will be Mikhail Kalinin's third run for president under the RKP. Despite his previous electoral defeats, he retains the loyalty of the party as well as the Communist international. ?]

Many republicans of the Müsavat Party, Kadets, PSR, and RSDRP float the idea of running Vladimir Nabokov, a nationally renowed lawyer, statesman, and Kadet, as a unity candidate. He has a history of working with moderate socialists, conservatives, and liberals, while also being ardently opposed to Solidarism.

Barring that, the Müsavat Party, Esers, and Kadets are planning to run separate candidates in the first round if the RSDRP runs its own candidate. The Kadets are considering nominating Dr. Aleksandr Konovalov, a businessman and member of the Kadet's right-wing. He calls for an alliance of Liberals and Conservatives that encompasses both Orthodox Democrats and Constitutional Monarchists. The Müsavat Party might nominate Mahammad Amin Rasulzade, their party leader, who has crossover appeal to Muslims and other regionalist parties who dislike the Russian-orientation of the mainstream parties.

As for the RSDRP, we could support a joint Socialist candidate or run our own.
[? if maisky_campaign : Ivan Maisky is tired. He ran and failed in the previous presidential election, and no longer wishes to run. He voiced his preference to be a diplomat over a president. ?] [? if maisky_campaign = 0 and rsdrp_prussia : Ivan Maisky is tired. He does not wish to run, but he is willing to be convinced that he is needed at this moment. ?]
Mikhail Yakubovich is an ambitious rising star in the party, and would be all too willing to run. He was a Bolshevik before the Great War and became a Menshevik because of the war, but his relatively young age might be relevant.
[? if womens_rights >= 6 and family_law >= 2 and womens_work >= 2 and welfare > 0: Or, perhaps it is time for a woman to become president. Germany and the RSDRP have been leading on women's rights for years, and it is only fitting that we should elect the world's first female president. Perhaps Eva Broido or Lyubov Radchenko could be seen as the "mother of the nation" for their work in social welfare. However, the very fact that either of the two are women may prove a challenge among the conservative population of Russia. ?]

[? if presidential_powers >= 1 and pro_republic >= 60 and pacifism >= 3: Since Russia has been largely democratized and presidential powers reduced, we can also support another non-traditional political figure, someone from the cultural, scientific, or literary spheres. They will perhaps be the symbol of a new Russia that has moved beyond its past. ?]

# options: 
# - Ivan Maisky (only if rsdrp_prussia)
# - Mikhail Yakubovich
# - Eva Broido/Lyubov Radchenko (The mother of the nation, only available if women's rights is full)

