@cultural_figure
view-if: presidential_powers >= 1 and pro_republic >= 60 and pacifism >= 3
subtitle: The next president should be a symbol of the new Germany...

Since the presidency has been made a largely ceremonial figure by our reforms, we might set a precedent by nominating a nonpartisan pro-democracy figure from the cultural, scientific, or literary worlds. There are many potential candidates...

- @pavlov_r1: Ivan Pavlov, the renowned scientist.
- @gorky_r1: Maxim Gorky, left-wing writer.
- @zaslavsky_r1: David Zaslavsky, former Bundist and non-partisan Social Democrat.
- @candidate_menu: Actually, we should nominate someone else.

@pavlov_r1
subtitle: -2 resources
view-if: science >= 2 and kwg_research >= 1 and (major_curriculum = "scientific" or minor_curriculum = "scientific")
choose-if: resources >= 2
on-arrival: rsdrp_candidate = "Pavlov"; resources -= 2; is_unity_candidate = 0; is_cultural_candidate = 1;
go-to: r1_menu

Ivan Pavlov is a Nobel Prize-winning scientist, director of the Physiological Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences, and disoverved Classical Conditioning.

He did not wish to run for president, preferring to remain in his position as a scientist, but we have somehow convinced him that his candidacy will be crucial to the future of Russian democracy and the world.

@gorky_r1
subtitle: -2 resources - He might be too far left for the bourgeois parties.
view-if: rkp_relation >= 50
choose-if: resources >= 2
on-arrival: rsdrp_candidate = "Gorky"; resources -= 2; is_unity_candidate = 0; is_cultural_candidate = 1;
go-to: r1_menu

Maxim Gorky is a world-renowned novelist and poet who is also known to be a left-wing activist. His political sympathy lies with the far left, and he supported the Bolsheviks for a short-while, before turning away from them due to their democratic backsliding.

Gorky is willing to accept the nomination for president.


@zaslavsky_r1
view-if: weltbuhne_dropped = 1
subtitle: -2 resources - He might be too far left for the bourgeois parties.
choose-if: resources >= 2
on-arrival: rsdrp_candidate = "Zaslavsky"; resources -= 2; is_unity_candidate = 0; is_cultural_candidate = 1;
go-to: r1_menu

David Zaslavsky is a left-wing journalist who is the publisher of the Coming Day, a Socialist magazine. He is most known for exposing the fact that Lenin as a paid German agent. In the 1932 presidential election, he endorsed the {!<span class="tooltip-text" title="Independent Socialist Bloc"><span style="color: #E48085;">NS Bloc</span></span>!} candidate, Semyon Semkovsky.

Zaslavsky is somewhat reluctant to become a candidate, citing his previous expulsion from the Bund and the RSDRP over corresponding with conservative generals during the civil war, as well as our status as one of the "mainstream" parties. It is with a sense of irony that he accepts our nomination.


